From 779f60f5d4a96e4558f6aea11d759d4b1279ab6d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Konrad Dybcio <konrad.dybcio@oss.qualcomm.com>
Date: Wed, 1 Jul 2026 18:33:10 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 47/85] PCI: Grow hotplug bridge windows to fit a hot-added
 switch subtree

When a hotplug bridge is populated, pci_assign_unassigned_bridge_resources()
sizes, distributes and assigns the resources of the hierarchy that appeared
behind it.  The sizing pass __pci_bus_size_bridges(parent) also covers the
hotplug bridge's own windows, because @parent is the bridge's secondary bus
whose ->self is the bridge itself.

However, pbus_size_mem() and pbus_size_io() return early for windows that
are already assigned (resource_assigned()).  The hotplug bridge's windows
were assigned at boot and sized to a single hotplug reservation
(pci_hotplug_mmio_size, default 2 MB).  They therefore stay frozen at that
boot-time size and are never re-placed into the parent aperture, even when
much more space is free there.

This breaks Thunderbolt/USB4 docks that contain a PCIe switch fanning out
to several downstream hotplug ports.  Each downstream port wants its own
hotplug reservation, so the demand is N * pci_hotplug_mmio_size, but the
upstream root port forwards only the one reservation-worth of space it was
frozen at.  The distributor can only subdivide that fixed window, so only
one downstream port gets a usable window and the rest fail with
"can't assign; no space" - while the host bridge aperture still has plenty
of room.  Example: a root port with a 2 MB window in front of a switch
with three active downstream ports (3 * 2 MB demand), backed by a host
bridge with 13 MB of MMIO and 64 GB of prefetchable space free.

Release the hotplug bridge's own windows before the sizing loop when they
hold no assigned resources.  Once unassigned, __pci_bus_size_bridges()
re-sizes them to cover the whole subtree and __pci_bridge_assign_resources()
re-places them into the larger available parent aperture, after which the
downstream ports are distributed within the grown window.

Only windows without assigned children are released, so an already
populated hierarchy (rescan, or a partially used bridge) is left
untouched; pci_bridge_release_resources() additionally guards on
resource_assigned().

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
Upstream-Status: Pending
---
 drivers/pci/setup-bus.c | 33 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 33 insertions(+)

diff --git a/drivers/pci/setup-bus.c b/drivers/pci/setup-bus.c
index ed16ef7c26fa..594d70a5aa6c 100644
--- a/drivers/pci/setup-bus.c
+++ b/drivers/pci/setup-bus.c
@@ -2267,6 +2267,39 @@ void pci_assign_unassigned_bridge_resources(struct pci_dev *bridge)
 	LIST_HEAD(fail_head);
 	int ret;
 
+	/*
+	 * If this is a hotplug bridge, release its own bridge windows before
+	 * sizing the hierarchy that has just appeared behind it.
+	 *
+	 * The sizing pass below (__pci_bus_size_bridges(parent)) also covers
+	 * this bridge's own windows, since @parent is the secondary bus whose
+	 * ->self is @bridge.  However, pbus_size_mem()/pbus_size_io() bail out
+	 * on windows that are already assigned, so a window keeps whatever size
+	 * it was given at boot - a single hotplug reservation
+	 * (pci_hotplug_mmio_size).  That is not enough when the hot-added
+	 * device is a switch fanning out to several downstream hotplug ports:
+	 * each port wants its own reservation, the sum exceeds the frozen
+	 * window, and only one port ends up with a usable window while free
+	 * parent aperture sits unused.
+	 *
+	 * Releasing the windows lets them be re-sized to fit the whole subtree
+	 * and re-placed into the larger available parent aperture.  Only
+	 * windows that hold no assigned resources are released, so an already
+	 * populated hierarchy (e.g. a rescan) is left undisturbed;
+	 * pci_bridge_release_resources() enforces that via resource_assigned().
+	 */
+	if (bridge->is_hotplug_bridge) {
+		int i;
+
+		for (i = 0; i < PCI_P2P_BRIDGE_RESOURCE_NUM; i++) {
+			struct resource *win =
+				pci_resource_n(bridge, PCI_BRIDGE_RESOURCES + i);
+
+			if (win->flags && !win->child)
+				pci_bridge_release_resources(parent, win);
+		}
+	}
+
 	while (1) {
 		__pci_bus_size_bridges(parent, &add_list);
 
